And she's gonna share
some of the questions.

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:59.780
Hopefully we'll be able
to answer them here.

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:03.643
- Awesome.

00:15:03.643 --> 00:15:05.210
Thank you so much, Roy,

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.850
for sharing that wonderful
information with us.

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:10.550
And just again, my name is Patty, here.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:13.290
I get the pleasure to be on

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.820
the district partnerships team with Roy.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:17.740
And so as part of the information

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:19.150
that Roy just shared with us,

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:20.750
we also wanna make sure we're grabbing

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:23.010
any of the questions you might have.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.540
And so throughout the question area,

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:27.977
I also saw people were asking,

00:15:27.977 --> 00:15:29.540
"Where can I get the slides?

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.150
Where can I look at the handout?"

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:34.910
And potentially, "Is this a webinar

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:36.790
that's gonna be recorded
and shared with us?"

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:39.570
So the answer to all those is, yes.

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:42.470
You will receive both the deck.

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:44.270
Slide deck or handout.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:46.480
And you'll also receive a
recording of the webinar

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.300
via email after our session.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:53.270
And then if you are in
your GoToWebinar screen,

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:56.290
there should be a little
tab that says handouts.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:58.500
And that tab will have a PDF

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.820
called Camp Khan article webinar.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:01.800
So if you click on that,

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:02.790
you'll then be able to see

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.880
all the information
that Roy shared as well.

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:07.730
So this would be a great time,

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:10.080
if you wanna add some pressing questions

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:11.450
that came up for you.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:14.010
I can see a lot of folks are
adding questions as well.

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:15.340
And we're gonna start out with one

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:18.920
that I also noticed
was pretty common, Roy.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:22.940
And it's specifically
coming to us from Carrie.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.650
And Carrie has actually already started

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:27.890
using Camp Khan this week.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:31.220
And so she's using it with her two kiddos.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:33.400
One of them is in fifth
grade, and seventh grade.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:34.750
And they're enjoying it.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:36.850
And they're wondering a little bit about

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:39.070
what the pace should be for a student

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:40.840
to kind of move through the content.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:43.450
So she's saying she wasn't really sure

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:44.777
what time to aim for.

00:16:44.777 --> 00:16:47.680
"Is there a recommendation or a time

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:51.330
that students should spend
weekly on Camp Khan?"

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:52.610
- Yeah, great question Carrie.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:53.870
And thanks for getting started

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.480
on the Get ready courses.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:58.723
And hope your girls are
enjoying the courses.

00:16:58.723 --> 00:17:02.430
We recommend probably about
two or three hours per week

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.883
on the Get ready for grade level courses.

00:17:05.883 --> 00:17:07.763
Now, you can split those out,

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.820
an hour a day for two days.

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:13.000
Or you can say, "I'm gonna do 30 minutes

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:16.780
or 45 minutes a day
for four or five days."

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:18.160
That's totally up to you.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.990
Again, we want this to be
as flexible as possible

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:22.500
given your schedules.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:26.500
And then in addition to
the hours spent per week,

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:28.640
we also recommend that students

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:31.600
make about five to 10
percentage point increase

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.563
in their mastery progress.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:36.500
- Awesome, thank you.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:38.500
I think that will be
really helpful for parents

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:41.396
as they're nudging and
encouraging students

00:17:41.396 --> 00:17:45.330
to work through their Get
ready for grade level courses.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.540
So a little bit more
on the logistics, Roy,

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:49.690
just to make sure
parents are really clear.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:52.469
And anyone who's joining us on the line.

00:17:52.469 --> 00:17:56.650
Stitcher wants to know, "Is
Camp Khan limited to math?"

00:17:56.650 --> 00:17:58.230
- Yeah, great question.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:02.290
So Camp Khan, the work that students

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.700
will be going through is limited

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:06.790
to the Get ready for grade level courses

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:08.810
which are in math.

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:10.225
At the same time,

00:18:10.225 --> 00:18:13.120
we're hoping that Camp Khan
is more than just math.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.550
We're hoping it's an opportunity

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.100
for students to build confidence.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:17.370
It's an opportunity for students

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:20.060
to have a little bit of
fun because it is summer.

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:21.880
So we're going to be infusing

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:24.350
those suggestions for progressing in math

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:26.580
on those Get ready for
grade level courses.

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:29.330
Along with say, some reflection prompts

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:31.997
that asks students to really think about,

00:18:31.997 --> 00:18:33.560
"How do I learn best?

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:36.540
What can I do when I
find myself struggling

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:37.600
with learning something?

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:41.417
How do I feel when I
finally do learn something

00:18:41.417 --> 00:18:43.120
that's really challenging."

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:45.240
So some of those learning reflections

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:47.090
and prompts that's gonna
build their confidence

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:48.580
and awareness as a learner.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.180
And then also some fun activities,

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:52.840
like getting outside and
going on nature walks.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.950
Or doing virtual tours through museums

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:58.690
that are offering these
great opportunities.

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:00.470
Or looking through some of our content

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:03.173
like Pixar in a box or
Imagineering in a box.

00:19:03.173 --> 00:19:04.923
It's really about creativity.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:07.680
- Thank you for sharing that.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:08.790
Yeah, I think it's great

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:12.410
to make sure there's a holistic set

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:15.300
of opportunities for students.

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:16.133
Awesome.

00:19:16.133 --> 00:19:18.750
So the next question
comes to us from Jeffrey.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:21.537
And Jeffrey wants to get super clear,

00:19:21.537 --> 00:19:25.180
"Do parents or students
pick the course level?

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:27.877
And can they switch if needed?"

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:30.830
- Great question, Jeffrey.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:33.490
Students will naturally
pick the course level.

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:35.730
Though we went past that parents

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.250
are just sitting alongside their child

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:39.993
as they pick the course level.

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.860
We do recommend that students

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:45.050
at least start with the grade level

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:46.740
that they're gonna be
entering in the fall.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:47.930
And your second question,

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:49.890
they can always switch if they want.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.980
They can always click in the other classes

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:54.090
in the other Get ready
for grade level courses.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:55.090
Depending on whether they find

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:58.133
the material too challenging or too easy.

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:02.900
- Great, I think that flexibility is key

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.500
for students and for parents as well.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.960
So let's switch the view a little bit

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:10.600
and think more about
the student themselves,

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.560
who's gonna be working on this content.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:17.560
And so when we think about
the design of the courses,

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:23.260
are they getting students to catch up

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:24.350
on their current grade level?

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:26.750
And this question is
specifically from (indistinct).

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:28.050
So just wannabe clear, (indistinct).

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.867
We're answering your question.

00:20:29.867 --> 00:20:31.530
"Are the Get ready classes designed

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:34.000
to help students catch up to
their current grade level?

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:37.580
Or can they also be used
for advanced learners,

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.550
and how could they be used
for advanced learners?"

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:41.383
- Yep.

00:20:41.383 --> 00:20:43.343
Again, another great question.

00:20:45.424 --> 00:20:47.190
The creation of the Get ready courses

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.960
was more to get students caught up.

00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:52.150
So more to your first question.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:53.780
We wanna make sure again,

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.990
that if you do the get
ready for let's say,

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:59.870
fifth grade course, you're
gonna enter fifth grade

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.213
ready to learn the fifth grade math.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:08.130
For advanced learners, will they be good?

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:09.810
I think the get ready courses

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:13.520
could potentially be good courses

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.340
for advanced learners too.

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.410
And I'm thinking particularly for,

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:20.470
if we have some middle school students,

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:23.880
middle school mathematician who

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:25.560
are really kind of chomping at the bit

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:28.450
to know a little bit
about high school math.

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.880
Or to think, "I wonder how
well I would do in algebra?"

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:35.130
They could take those
high school level courses,

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.350
just to get a sense of
what they would need

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:39.691
to be successful in those courses.

00:21:39.691 --> 00:21:42.260
So short answer is for advanced learners,

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:45.020
I would recommend even
going on to grade levels

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:48.193
above where a student is going
to be entering in the fall.

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:50.913
- That's a great.

00:21:50.913 --> 00:21:51.770
That's a great suggestion.

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:53.180
And as we're thinking about

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:55.010
students potentially looking

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:58.880
or reviewing content and
potentially getting stuck, right?

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:00.050
We know that that happens.

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:02.340
And that's okay when you're learning math.

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:05.060
So how could kids seek help?

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:07.440
And this is a question from Nepor,

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:09.080
who wants to know...

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:11.100
Or references that you mentioned, Roy

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.960
that there's gonna be an
option to seek guidance.

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:16.390
So how could students get help?

00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:17.954
- Yep, great question Nepor.

00:22:17.954 --> 00:22:21.510
In each of our exercises in Khan Academy,

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:25.260
there are videos students can watch

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:30.260
that gives them a teaching
of what the concept is.

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:32.960
And in addition, there are hints

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:35.210
that will give students
kind of step by step

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:37.620
how you solve this problem.

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:39.170
We always recommend that students

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:40.400
watch the video first

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:43.230
'cause that probably
just about the concept.

00:22:43.230 --> 00:22:44.540
And we've also seen some students

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:46.210
just watch the videos before

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:49.063
they even start on their problems.

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:54.830
In addition to the videos,
if student is really stuck

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:57.060
they can always just click the hint

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.400
under each question and the hint will be

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:00.680
a step by step solving

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:04.020
of that particular question
the student is seeing.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:07.170
And we think this is a
great way for students to

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.110
again, take a little bit of ownership

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:11.720
or more ownership in their learning.

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:13.610
And also, we hope this is a way

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:15.980
where a student is constantly

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:17.570
gonna be asking you the parent

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:19.520
for help every time you might be stuck.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:22.410
- That's really helpful.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:25.870
And a similar question from Deanna,

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:27.130
relate it to that, right?

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:30.650
Like, "Do parents need
to be hands on helping?

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:33.650
Or is it fairly simple for
kids to navigate on their own?"

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:35.260
So maybe just sharing a little bit more

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:38.000
about how kids can navigate on their own.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:39.967
- Yeah, great question.

00:23:39.967 --> 00:23:42.760
The question of whether or how much

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:44.170
parents should be hands on,

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:46.610
we leave it totally up to the parent.

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:48.990
Again, we want Camp Khan and our courses

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:51.150
to be pretty flexible and ready to promote

00:23:52.931 --> 00:23:55.360
self driven learning but from the student.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:59.620
So for a student when they
get on the Khan Academy,

00:23:59.620 --> 00:24:01.780
we do recommend starting
with that course challenge.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:03.230
And then the exercises,

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:06.830
we always recommend that
follow a blue button.

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:08.851
So there's a blue Start button,

00:24:08.851 --> 00:24:11.020
or a blue Continue button.

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:12.162
And once you click on those

00:24:12.162 --> 00:24:15.520
then students will get
underway on their exercises.

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.770
And then once in the exercises,

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:19.726
they can start looking at those videos

00:24:19.726 --> 00:24:21.540
and viewing those hints.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:22.980
And then if they're stuck,

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:24.890
we can recommend that they asked

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:26.320
maybe an older sibling.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:30.330
Or they ask another adult
in the in their household,

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:34.290
and then eventually we might ask that

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:35.883
they reach out to the parent.

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:39.080
- I think that's great scaffolding.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:40.940
I think very similar to the way teachers

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:45.140
have the three before me rule
sometimes in the classroom.

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:47.320
So maybe parents caregivers out there

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:48.870
can do the three before me,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.490
and the first one can definitely be Khan

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.870
and the resources that you mentioned, Roy.

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:55.280
- Yeah.
- Similar to that,

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:59.297
I think as part of being
a parent, Telacy asks,

00:24:59.297 --> 00:25:03.110
"How do we as parents see
the progress of our students

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:04.730
and what they're learning?"

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:06.930
- Yeah, then a great question.

